\title{Fast and Easy Compilation of Vascular Plants Occurrence Records from GBIF}
\description{
\if{html}{\figure{logo.png}{options: width="120" alt="logo" style="float: right"}}

GBIF hosts over 500 million tracheophyte (vascular plant) occurrence
records, but processing them typically involves complex workflows and
substantial computational overhead. \strong{VasGBIF} is a fast, plant-optimised
R package that parses, validates, and consolidates GBIF occurrence records
into analysis-ready datasets.

VasGBIF integrates taxonomic resolution via the \link[TNRS:TNRS]{TNRS}, spatial validation
via \link[CoordinateCleaner:CoordinateCleaner]{CoordinateCleaner}, and native-range annotation against WCVP and
WGSRPD within a single high-performance pipeline. Its vectorised design
and selective parallelisation let it process one million records on a
laptop in under 15 minutes without specialised hardware.
}
\details{
\subsection{Pipeline overview}{

VasGBIF provides a reproducible workflow organised into eight sequential
steps. Each step progressively filters records through taxonomic, quality,
and coordinate checks, transforming GBIF occurrence records into
analysis-ready datasets.
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Import Records} — \code{\link[=import_records]{import_records()}}: reads a GBIF occurrence
download ZIP ('SIMPLE_CSV' or Darwin Core Archive), extracts the
occurrence table, and returns an \code{"import"} data.table of the fields
required by the workflow. No records are filtered at this step — all
diagnostic flags are preserved for later quality scoring.
\item \strong{Extract GBIF Issues} — \code{\link[=extract_gbif_issues]{extract_gbif_issues()}}: expands the raw
pipe-separated \code{issue} column into one logical indicator column per GBIF
issue code, plus a companion summary ranking issues by how many records
they flag.
\item \strong{Check Taxon Name} — \code{\link[=check_taxon]{check_taxon()}}: submits species- and
infraspecific-rank names to the Taxonomic Name Resolution Service (TNRS;
Boyle et al. 2013) for resolution against the World Checklist of
Vascular Plants (WCVP) or World Flora Online (WFO). Synonyms are
resolved to accepted names; records that fail the match-score threshold
or lack an accepted/synonym status are excluded from the downstream
table and reported in the \code{summary} for manual review.
\item \strong{Custom Filter} — \code{\link[=custom_filter]{custom_filter()}}: joins the imported records with
the resolved taxonomy and the parsed issue flags, then applies the
enabled filter rules (country code, coordinate uncertainty, GBIF issue
count, event date, collector and identifier fields) to retain only
high-quality records. Every rule is independently toggleable, and each
step is recorded in a per-rule audit table (see \emph{Flexible and fluent
custom filter system}).
\item \strong{Refine Coordinates} — \code{\link[=refine_coordinates]{refine_coordinates()}}: validates coordinates
with \code{\link[CoordinateCleaner:clean_coordinates]{CoordinateCleaner::clean_coordinates()}} (Zizka et al. 2019) to
flag spatial errors such as centroids, capitals, marine coordinates, and
zero coordinates, splitting records into cleaned, problematic, and
coordinate-less tables. Validation is parallelized across user-specified
threads.
\item \strong{Detect Native Status} — \code{\link[=detect_native_status]{detect_native_status()}}: matches each record
against WCVP distribution data (the internal \code{Distributions} dataset)
via WGSRPD Level 3 areas to classify it as native, introduced, extinct,
location_doubtful, or unknown (see \emph{Precise native-status detection
system}).
\item \strong{Export Records} — \code{\link[=export_records]{export_records()}}: writes the classified records
to disk as two gzip-compressed CSV files: all usable records and the
native subset.
\item \strong{Map Records} — \code{\link[=map_records]{map_records()}}: renders refined records on
interactive maps via \code{\link[mapview:mapView]{mapView()}}, with geohash-based
decluttering to reduce visual overlap. Records are colour-coded by
native status, and multiple basemap layers are supported (OpenStreetMap,
Esri World Imagery, and others).
}
}

\subsection{Precise native-status detection system}{

\code{\link[=detect_native_status]{detect_native_status()}} is the analytical core of VasGBIF: it assigns a
native, introduced, extinct, location_doubtful, or unknown classification
to every occurrence by matching the record's identification and position
against authoritative WCVP distribution data (the internal \code{Distributions}
dataset) organised by WGSRPD Level 3 areas. Classification runs in two
passes so that the most precise available evidence always wins.

\strong{Spatial pass.} Records with validated coordinates are overlaid on the
WGSRPD Level 3 polygon map with \code{\link[terra:extract]{terra::extract()}} — a single vectorised
call that assigns every point its area code in compiled code. Each area
code is looked up in a distribution table classified from the WCVP flags
(\code{introduced}, \code{extinct}, \code{location_doubtful}) with a fixed priority:
\enumerate{
\item \code{location_doubtful} wins over every other flag;
\item otherwise \code{introduced};
\item otherwise \code{extinct};
\item otherwise, when no flag is set, the area is \code{native};
\item any remaining case is \code{unknown}.
}

A record that falls in several areas at once receives the most preferred
status (\code{native} first), never an arbitrary one. Coastal points that fall
just outside a polygon are still matched through a geodesic buffer
(\code{buffer_km}, applied in metres by \code{\link[terra:buffer]{terra::buffer()}} so its meaning is
identical at every latitude); buffered hits always rank below exact ones,
so a genuine in-polygon match is never displaced by a buffered candidate.

\strong{Country-code pass.} Records without coordinates, and records the
spatial pass left unresolved, are retried through \code{countryCode} mapped to
WGSRPD Level 3 areas by the \code{Level3maping} table. No geometry is used, so
this pass is nearly free, and buffering is chunked (\code{buffer_chunk_size})
to keep the relate matrix small.

Every classification records how it was obtained in
\code{native_status_source} — \code{spatial} / \code{spatial_buffered} for spatial
matches (exact and buffered), \code{country_code} /
\code{country_code_after_spatial_miss} for country-code matches,
\code{country_code_no_entry} when the country mapped but the taxon has no
distribution entry there, and \code{unmatched} when no usable key exists — so
the entire decision chain is auditable.

\strong{Precision without a speed penalty.} Hybrid markers are normalised so
\verb{Alnus x pubescens} matches the \verb{Alnus × pubescens} recorded in the
distributions; and neither the spatial overlay nor the distribution
lookup ever iterates record-by-record in R. The result is a
\code{nativeDetected} table keyed by \code{gbifID} that retains every column of the
input records and appends \code{LEVEL3_COD}, \code{native_status},
\code{native_status_source}, and \code{buffered}, feeding directly into
\code{\link[=export_records]{export_records()}} and \code{\link[=map_records]{map_records()}} without any join back to
\code{refined_coordinates}.
}

\subsection{Flexible and fluent custom filter system}{

\code{\link[=custom_filter]{custom_filter()}} turns the raw download into an analysis-ready
occurrence table. It joins the three preceding outputs (\code{occ_import},
\code{taxa_checked}, \code{gbif_issue}) into one table, then walks a user-selected
set of quality rules — one vectorised \link[data.table:data.table]{data.table} pass per rule — with
every step audited.

\strong{Fluent rule control.} Each rule is an independently toggleable
argument. Three rules are on by default (\code{countryCode},
\code{coordinateUncertainty <= 10000} m, \code{gbif_issues_max <= 5}), giving
sensible out-of-the-box behaviour; \code{date}, \code{identifiedBy}, and
\code{recordedBy} are opt-in, so no information is discarded without an
explicit choice. Numeric thresholds share one uniform "off" convention —
\code{NULL}, \code{NA}, or \code{''} — so any rule can be disabled without restructuring
the call.

\strong{Auditable pipeline.} Every step — the \code{taxon_resolved} join as well as
each enabled rule — is logged in the returned \code{summary} table (\code{rule},
\code{dropped}, \code{remaining}), making the effect of each decision visible and
reproducible. The joins are deliberately strict: the taxonomic join drops
unresolved names instead of carrying \code{NA} taxonomy forward, and the
one-to-one issue join is verified at runtime, stopping if any record
lacks an issue count.

\strong{Careful collector and identifier detection.} The \code{identifiedBy} and
\code{recordedBy} rules remove only values that contain no named person:
missing or empty values, a curated multilingual keyword list (\code{unknown},
\code{anonymous}, \code{desconocido}, \code{inconnu}, \code{unbekannt}, plus Chinese terms for
"unknown", "unnamed", "anonymous" and "no details"), and whole-value
patterns such as \code{s.n.} and \code{n/a}. Name separators (\verb{,}, \verb{;}, \code{&}, \code{+},
\code{and}, and their full-width CJK equivalents) protect values that mix a
keyword with a real name, and word-boundary matching keeps the CJK
keywords from splitting genuine names — deliberately conservative so
that real records are never dropped.

The output \code{customFiltered} object carries every downstream column in
\code{occ_filtered} and a per-rule \code{summary} that exposes the whole filtering
decision chain at a glance.
}

\subsection{Quick start}{

The built-in example dataset lets you run the full pipeline without a
GBIF download:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{library(VasGBIF)


gbif_file <- system.file(
  "extdata", "0003386-260721160103020.zip",
  package = "VasGBIF"
)

occ_import <- import_records(path = gbif_file)


gbif_issue <- extract_gbif_issues(occ_import)


taxa_checked <- check_taxon(occ_import = occ_import, accuracy = 0.85)


filtered <- custom_filter(
  occ_import = occ_import,
  taxa_checked = taxa_checked,
  gbif_issue = gbif_issue
)


refined_coordinates <- refine_coordinates(
  custom_filtered = filtered,
  threads = 4
)

native_detected <- detect_native_status(
  refined_coordinates = refined_coordinates
)


export_records(
  native_detected = native_detected,
  export_path = getwd()
)

map_records(
  native_detected = native_detected,
  precision = 3,
  cex = 3
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\subsection{Performance}{

VasGBIF achieves its speed through several architectural choices:
\itemize{
\item \strong{C/C++ backends}: core operations are delegated to \link[data.table:data.table]{data.table},
\link[stringi:stringi]{stringi}, and \link[terra:terra]{terra} — packages written in C/C++ that bypass R's
per-iteration interpretive overhead.
\item \strong{Vectorisation over explicit loops}: operations such as issue-flag
detection in \code{\link[=extract_gbif_issues]{extract_gbif_issues()}} and native-status lookups in
\code{\link[=detect_native_status]{detect_native_status()}} process entire columns in compiled calls rather
than iterating in R.
\item \strong{SIMD exploitation}: vectorised routines in \link[stringi:stringi]{stringi} and
\code{\link[terra:extract]{terra::extract()}} allow the compiler to emit SIMD
instructions (AVX, AVX-512) that process multiple data elements per CPU
cycle.
\item \strong{Memory-efficient design}: \link[data.table:data.table]{data.table}'s in-place modification \verb{:=}
avoids unnecessary copies, and contiguous memory access patterns improve
CPU cache utilisation.
\item \strong{Selective parallelisation}: \code{\link[=refine_coordinates]{refine_coordinates()}} partitions the
dataset into chunks and distributes \link[CoordinateCleaner:CoordinateCleaner]{CoordinateCleaner} validation
across workers via \link[foreach:foreach]{foreach} and \link[doParallel:doParallel]{doParallel}, combining vectorised
processing within each chunk with parallel execution across chunks.
}

On a standard laptop, VasGBIF compiles one million occurrence records
within 15 minutes.
}
